The bundled launcher: what the .exe actually carries
---------------------------------------------------

This paragraph used to say the launcher "downloads an official Node.js runtime
at first run ... it is not redistributed in this repository". That is true of
the repository and NOT true of the file people are handed. tools/launcher
embeds the Node zip in the exe by default (-NoEmbedNode is the opt-out), and
the payload also vendors sharp's prebuilt @img/* binaries, which contain
libvips. So the CalGamesContentDesk.exe handed to a volunteer does redistribute
both, and the file whose job is to state the licensing position should say so.

  Node.js (https://nodejs.org) - MIT License. An official win-x64 build,
    unmodified, verified against a pinned SHA-256 at build time. Its licence
    text ships in the payload as third-party/NODE-LICENSE.txt.
  libvips (https://libvips.org), via sharp's @img/* prebuilt binaries -
    LGPL-3.0-or-later. Unmodified upstream binaries, dynamically linked by
    sharp. Its licence text ships as third-party/LIBVIPS-LICENSE.txt, and the
    corresponding source is available from libvips.org.

Build with -NoEmbedNode and the exe carries neither: Node is fetched on first
run instead, and the licence texts go with whatever is actually included.
Node.js is MIT licensed.
